{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And if his offering is a goat.</b> Scripture mentioned the species — if his offering is from the species of goats — in accordance with the opinion of Onkelos (Onkelos on Leviticus 3:12), who said \"מן בני עזיא\" (of the sons of goats), and likewise what it says concerning the olah (Leviticus 1:10), \"from the sheep or from the goats\" — meaning from their species. In my opinion, the language of the sacred text is not particular about the names of animals, for in most of them one name serves for both male and female, as גמל (camel) and חמור (donkey) and שפן (hyrax) and ארנבת (hare) and חזיר (pig), and among birds יונה (dove) and תור (turtledove). And even among the species that have distinct names for male and female, such as שור (ox) and פרה (cow), כשב (ram) and כשבה (ewe), תיש (male goat) and עז (female goat), it is not particular, and it says שה (lamb) and שור (ox) for a female, as in the verse (Leviticus 22:28), \"And whether it be ox or sheep (שור או שה), you shall not kill it and its young together on one day\" — and this applies only to a cow and an ewe, according to the opinion of the one who says: we do not take concern for the father's seed (Hullin 78a). And likewise, \"from the goats, a male without blemish you shall offer it\" (Leviticus 1:10) — from the male goats; \"and if his offering is a goat\" (Leviticus 3:12) — male or female; and likewise, \"And if his means do not suffice for a sheep (שה)\" (Leviticus 5:7) — an ewe or a female goat."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The phrase אין חוששין לזרע האב is a Talmudic principle (Hullin 78a) holding that the prohibition of killing an animal and its young on the same day applies only when both share the same mother, not the same father."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:50:10.568609739Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}